# Accessibility

Applies when **building or changing UI**. Load for components, pages,
forms. Not every chat. The floor is WCAG 2.2 AA — not a ceiling, a floor.

## Keyboard

- Every interactive element is reachable and operable via keyboard.
- Visible focus on every focusable element; never remove the outline
  without replacing it.
- Logical tab order; no keyboard traps (a modal must trap and release
  cleanly).

## Semantics & ARIA

- Use native semantics first (`button`, `nav`, `dialog`). Add ARIA only
  where native semantics are missing or insufficient.
- `aria-label` / `aria-labelledby` on icon-only buttons and ambiguous
  controls; never duplicate a visible label as an `aria-label`.
- Live regions (`aria-live`) for dynamic updates that a screen-reader user
  would otherwise miss.

## Labels & names

- Every input has a visible `<label>` (or equivalent). No
  `placeholder`-as-label.
- Every interactive element has an accessible name.
- Required fields indicated programmatically (`required`, `aria-required`),
  not by color or text alone.

## Contrast & motion

- Text contrast ≥ 4.5:1 (normal), ≥ 3:1 (large). UI components and
  graphical objects ≥ 3:1.
- Respect `prefers-reduced-motion`; no autoplaying motion that can't be
  paused.

## Do not

- Ship a UI change without a keyboard check.
- Use color or icon alone to convey state — add text or `aria-label`.
- Add ARIA that duplicates native semantics (redundant `role="button"` on
  a `<button>`).

